#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""qbit-ctl: operate the QBIT backend data volume from the host.

Full-screen console for the production server, plus a few plain commands for scripts and cron.
Standard library only (Python 3.10+, curses); everything that touches the data runs inside Docker.

    qbit-ctl                      interactive console
    qbit-ctl status               backend, volume, database and latest backup
    qbit-ctl backup [--dir DIR]   consistent backup without stopping the backend
    qbit-ctl backups [--dir DIR]  list backup archives

Backup directory, first match wins: --dir, $QBIT_CTL_BACKUP_DIR, `dir` under [backups] in
$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/qbit-ctl/config.ini (~/.config/...; the console's `c` key can write it), ~/qbit_backups.

Design notes
- The database is read and written by the backend image itself (node + better-sqlite3), through
  `docker exec` while the backend runs and a throwaway container from the same image when it is
  stopped. No sqlite3 CLI is installed at run time, results come back as JSON (NULLs, `|`, tabs
  and CJK survive), and the SQLite version always matches the one the backend uses.
- Backups snapshot the database with VACUUM INTO (one read transaction), so they are consistent
  while the backend keeps serving. The archive layout is unchanged: qbit.db, secrets.json, files/.
- Restore validates the archive first (extract + integrity_check in a scratch container without the
  live volume), stops the backend, saves a safety backup of the current data, empties the volume,
  extracts, and starts the backend again. If the extract fails, the safety backup is put back.
  There is no "restore while running" path, and Ctrl-C / a dropped ssh session cannot interrupt it.
"""

import argparse
import configparser
